## 2026-08-22 — Session: the patient sign-in flow, and the docs that described it wrongly

### The reported bug

A patient signing in to the portal was landing on the full-page chat, having clicked
nothing. Tracing it end to end found two independent causes, not one:

- `PortalChatController::render()` emitted the AI Chat panel with a live
  `src="…/oauth/launch"`. TICK-032 had put that panel inside a Bootstrap `.collapse`,
  but a hidden iframe still loads — so rendering the dashboard started an OAuth flow for
  a panel nobody had opened. OpenEMR's OAuth2 provider keeps a session separate from the
  portal's, so it rendered a second sign-in *inside* the hidden panel, and TICK-045's
  breakout script dutifully navigated the top-level window off the dashboard.
- `/oauth/callback` then answered `303 -> AI_SESSION_SUCCESS_REDIRECT_URI`, which was the
  standalone chat page.

### Decisions

**The chat is a panel, never a landing page (ADR-8, FR-31).** An authorization completing
at top level lands on the portal dashboard; one completing inside the panel loads the
chat there. The patient is never returned to the page they were on, and no `next=` or
`redirect=` parameter may influence the destination.

The rule is stated over the flow's **position**, not the patient's intent. Two earlier
drafts tried to bind it to "did the patient enter credentials?" and both were wrong: a
live provider session completes the whole exchange with no prompt at all, so the callback
cannot know. Position is knowable — `Sec-Fetch-Dest` is `document` for a top-level
navigation and `iframe` for one into a frame — and it is what actually decides whether
landing on a full-page chat would strand the patient.

**Nothing authorizes until the patient opens the chat (FR-32).** The panel holds its URL
in `data-src` and promotes it on `show.bs.collapse`. This is also what makes the
destination rule safe to state absolutely: a dashboard that spawns no chat iframe at
render cannot be redirected into itself.

**The redirect target and the chat origin allowlist are two separately named settings.**
`AI_SESSION_SUCCESS_REDIRECT_URI` was doing both jobs, and the second is the only CSRF
defense on `POST /api/chat` — the session cookie is `SameSite=None` for the cross-site
iframe. Repointing the destination at the dashboard would have silently made
`emr.localhost` the only origin allowed to call the chat API. Split into
`AI_SESSION_DASHBOARD_REDIRECT_URI` and `AI_SESSION_CHAT_ORIGIN`, renamed rather than
reused so a deployment still carrying the old variable fails to boot.

**Portal sign-out now ends the AI session.** There was no logout at all: portal logout
ended OpenEMR's PHP session but could not reach `ai_session` on the chat origin, so
navigating back to the chat resumed the previous patient's session against a live
delegated token. `SessionStore.purge_expired()` also existed but was never called, so
sessions abandoned by closing the browser kept their encrypted tokens indefinitely.

**The 8-hour TTL stays absolute, and is announced.** Redeeming the stored refresh token
was considered and deliberately declined: sliding renewal on a delegated medical-record
token is a policy question, and it could not be honestly verified inside a privacy fix.

### Also corrected

The merge gate in `GIT_WORKFLOW_COIDH.md` claimed the project had no test suite and told
agents not to write tests — while CI ran `pytest` over 34 test files and NFR-18 requires
80% coverage. `BACKLOG.md`'s traceability table had stopped at TICK-031, omitting 27
tickets; it is now regenerated by `scripts/verify_backlog_traceability.py` and gated in
CI, so it cannot silently rot again.

### Corrections to this session's own work

Three claims recorded here were wrong when first written and are worth keeping visible:
`/oauth/launch` was described as "not patient-facing" when it is the panel's own `src`;
the probe scripts were said to depend on its top-level behaviour when none of them touch
it; and FR ids were assigned as FR-36/FR-37 into a gap that did not exist, because a
`FR-[0-9]+` search matches inside `NFR-31`–`NFR-35`. The last of those is why the new
traceability check anchors its requirement pattern.

### Still open

TICK-025 (Android acceptance) is blocked on emulator access. TICK-056 asks whether
OpenEMR's OAuth2 provider can accept an existing portal session, which decides whether
opening the chat costs the patient a second sign-in.
